'' ports.tiko -- fb_hIn and fb_hOut, the INP and OUT statements' back end. Two
'' symbols, one fbc member (win32/sys_portio.o on win64, unix/sys_portio.o on
'' linux-x86_64).
''
'' LINUX SINCE 2026-09-19 (decision 0015, D15). Until then this file was empty
'' there and fbc's own member came from lib/linux-x86_64-fb/. The permission
'' check is here; the two instructions are src/c/portinsn.c -- the measurement
'' below, that -gen llvm cannot emit them, still stands.
''
'' NAMED ports.tiko RATHER THAN portio.bas ON PURPOSE. The build flattens
'' src/sys/portio.bas to sys_portio.o, which is fbc's OWN member name, and
'' gate.sh merges by deleting fbc members that collide. On win32 and linux this
'' file is EMPTY -- so the merge would have deleted fbc's real sys_portio.o and
'' put an empty object in its place, silently removing fb_hIn and fb_hOut from
'' the archive on the two targets that still need fbc's versions. build.sh
'' refuses the collision by name; the rename is the fix.
''
'' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
'' WIN32 AND LINUX CANNOT BE DONE, AND THIS TIME THAT IS MEASURED RATHER THAN
'' INHERITED FROM A TABLE.
''
'' The hinit.o batch found the "stays in C" classification wrong because the
'' inline asm it cited was behind a #if arm that no target in R3 compiles. That
'' made every remaining Class A entry worth re-checking rather than trusting.
'' sys_portio.o was re-checked the same way, and it comes out the OTHER way:
''
''   win32   HOST_X86 is defined, so fb_hIn/fb_hOut really do contain
''             __asm__ volatile ("inb %1, %0" : "=a" (value) : "d" (port))
''           and the outb counterpart. The shipped object proves the arm is live
''           -- it carries the whole driver-installation machinery
''           (OpenSCManager, CreateService, DeviceIoControl, the 5,632-byte
''           fbportio.sys blob as `fbportio_driver`), which only exists inside
''           that same #ifdef.
''   linux   `#if defined HOST_X86 || defined HOST_X86_64` -- x86-64 satisfies
''           the second half, so the asm is live there too.
''   win64   HOST_X86 is NOT defined, so the member falls through to the #else
''           arm: two one-line stubs that report ILLEGALFUNCTIONCALL. The object
''           confirms it -- fb_hIn is 0x20 bytes and the ONLY thing it imports
''           is fb_ErrorSetNum. No driver, no service handles, no blob.
''
'' AND -gen llvm REALLY DOES REJECT INLINE ASM. Probed rather than assumed,
'' because the whole point of re-checking is not to trade one inherited belief
'' for another:
''
''     asmprobe.bas(9) error 374: Inline ASM is not supported by -gen llvm;
''     compile this module with -gen gas64 or -gen gcc
''
'' There is no API alternative to the instruction, either. The driver does not
'' PERFORM the I/O -- IOCTL_GRANT_IOPM edits the process's I/O permission bitmap
'' so that a user-mode `inb` stops faulting. The instruction is the mechanism,
'' not an optimisation of one.
''
'' So win32 keeps three symbols in C (fb_hIn, fb_hOut, fbportio_driver) and
'' linux keeps two. That is the correct answer for those targets, and it is
'' recorded here so the next re-check reads the measurement instead of repeating
'' it.
''
'' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
'' THE win64 STUBS ARE NOT A DEGRADED PORT -- they are what fbc ships. INP and
'' OUT are unavailable in every 64-bit Windows build of FreeBASIC, and the
'' program is told so through the normal error path rather than by a crash.
''
'' THE SIGNS DIFFER, DELIBERATELY. fb_hIn NEGATES the error number and fb_hOut
'' does not. fb_hIn's return value is the BYTE READ, so it has no spare
'' encoding for failure -- a negative result is the out-of-band signal, and 0..255
'' stays a valid reading. fb_hOut has nothing to return but a status, so the
'' error number is the result. Copying one line to the other would give INP a
'' plausible reading of 1 on a machine that cannot do port I/O at all.
''
'' NEITHER IS FBCALL. fb_system.h declares both plainly, and fbc's own win32
'' object names them `_fb_hIn` / `_fb_hOut` -- the C leading underscore with no
'' @N suffix, which is what settles it.

#include once "inc/rterror.ti"

'' WINDOWS, macOS AND NON-x86 LINUX: no user-mode port I/O, so INP and OUT are an illegal
'' function call (docs/tiko-macos M5, D-M5-1). linux-arm64 is here by fbc's own rule: fbc
'' 1.10.1's linux/sys_portio.c is '#if HOST_X86 || HOST_X86_64 ... #else return
'' fb_ErrorSetNum( FB_RTERROR_ILLEGALFUNCTIONCALL )', and natively in the Lima VM INP gave -1
'' and OUT aborted with runtime error 1 under -exx (docs/tiko-linux-arm64 L2).
'' ANDROID TOO, x86 or not: Bionic has no ioperm and an app no port permission, and fbc's
'' rtlib asks for one on x86 glibc Linux only (docs/tiko-android A1).
#if defined( __FB_WIN32__ ) or defined( __FB_DARWIN__ ) or defined( __FB_ANDROID__ ) or ( not defined( __FB_X86__ ) )

extern "rtlib"   '' FBCALL.
	declare function tkrt_ErrorSetNum4 alias "fb_ErrorSetNum" ( byval e as int32 ) as int32
end extern

extern "c"   '' NON-FBCALL: fb_system.h declares both plainly.

function tkort_hIn alias "fb_hIn" ( byval port as uint16 ) as int32
	return -tkrt_ErrorSetNum4( FB_RTERROR_ILLEGALFUNCTIONCALL )
end function

function tkort_hOut alias "fb_hOut" _
	( byval port as uint16, byval value_ as uint8 ) as int32
	return tkrt_ErrorSetNum4( FB_RTERROR_ILLEGALFUNCTIONCALL )
end function

end extern

#elseif defined( __FB_LINUX__ )

'' x86 LINUX, and only that: the instructions are src/c/portinsn.c, compiled for
'' linux-x86_64 alone, and the permission comes from ioperm, which is x86 Linux's.
'' Another POSIX target must choose its own answer (M5) rather than inherit an
'' unresolved tkort_inb. fbc's unix/sys_portio.c, read for its contract (reading is permitted --
'' lib/rt/PROVENANCE.md): no permission is NOPRIVILEGES, negated for fb_hIn for
'' the reason given above; with it, the instruction and FB_RTERROR_OK. has_perm
'' is set once, in fb_hInit, from ioperm( 0, &h400, 1 ) -- so it is FALSE for any
'' process that is not root, which is the usual case.

#include once "inc/console.ti"

extern "rtlib"   '' FBCALL.
	declare function tkrt_ErrorSetNum4 alias "fb_ErrorSetNum" ( byval e as int32 ) as int32
end extern

extern "c"   '' NON-FBCALL: src/c/portinsn.c, and fb_system.h's two below.

declare function tkort_inb( byval port as uint16 ) as uint8
declare sub tkort_outb( byval port as uint16, byval value_ as uint8 )

function tkort_hIn alias "fb_hIn" ( byval port as uint16 ) as int32
	if( TKORT_CON_L( TKORT_CON_HASPERM ) = 0 ) then
		return -tkrt_ErrorSetNum4( FB_RTERROR_NOPRIVILEGES )
	end if
	return tkort_inb( port )
end function

function tkort_hOut alias "fb_hOut" _
	( byval port as uint16, byval value_ as uint8 ) as int32
	if( TKORT_CON_L( TKORT_CON_HASPERM ) = 0 ) then
		return tkrt_ErrorSetNum4( FB_RTERROR_NOPRIVILEGES )
	end if
	tkort_outb( port, value_ )
	return FB_RTERROR_OK
end function

end extern

#else
	#error "sys/ports: no port I/O backend for this OS"
#endif
